WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:07.156
This is Play the Recorder from Zero, by FlautaQuest. The soprano recorder, one step at a time.

00:00:07.956 --> 00:00:10.805
Today: first songs and practice and care.

00:00:12.605 --> 00:00:15.347
First songs. Putting it all together.

00:00:16.147 --> 00:00:28.533
Now you have enough notes for some well-known melodies. The two below are in the public domain: they are free for everyone to play. Read each line slowly first, saying the note names, and then play it.

00:00:30.033 --> 00:00:30.903
Ode to Joy.

00:00:31.703 --> 00:00:41.588
The famous theme from the last movement of Beethoven’s Ninth Symphony. It moves mostly step by step, which makes it perfect for practising smooth finger changes.

00:00:42.388 --> 00:00:44.773
The example is written out in the ebook.

00:00:45.573 --> 00:00:58.516
In the fourth and eighth bars, B. A means a longer B, a beat and a half, followed by a short A, half a beat. Together they take two beats. If you know the tune, your ear will guide you.

00:01:00.016 --> 00:01:00.916
Frère Jacques.

00:01:01.716 --> 00:01:11.258
A traditional round. Once you know it, two or more players can play it as a canon: the second player starts from the beginning when the first one reaches the second line.

00:01:12.058 --> 00:01:14.443
The example is written out in the ebook.

00:01:15.243 --> 00:01:24.872
In the third line, D’E’D’C’ are quick notes, two per beat. That line uses the pinched high E from chapter 6; the last line uses the low D.

00:01:26.372 --> 00:01:27.806
Playing with expression.

00:01:28.606 --> 00:01:31.433
When the notes are secure, think about the music:

00:01:32.233 --> 00:01:38.274
Phrases: a song is made of musical sentences. Breathe between them, not in the middle.

00:01:39.074 --> 00:01:44.408
Smoothness: in Ode to Joy, use a very soft “du” so the notes join together smoothly.

00:01:45.208 --> 00:01:52.947
Dynamics: the recorder cannot get much louder or softer without going out of tune, so let the expression come from phrasing and tonguing.

00:01:53.747 --> 00:02:00.170
Tip: Record yourself on a phone and listen back. You will hear things you cannot notice while you play.

00:02:01.670 --> 00:02:02.402
Practice.

00:02:03.202 --> 00:02:10.116
First: learn Ode to Joy line by line. Play each line three times without mistakes before moving on.

00:02:10.916 --> 00:02:16.007
Second: learn Frère Jacques. Practise the third line slowly on its own.

00:02:16.807 --> 00:02:22.387
Third: play Frère Jacques as a round with a friend, a family member or a recording of yourself.

00:02:24.187 --> 00:02:28.559
Practice and care. Getting better and keeping your recorder healthy.

00:02:29.359 --> 00:02:37.858
Progress on the recorder comes from regular practice, and a well-kept recorder sounds better and lasts for years. This chapter covers both.

00:02:39.358 --> 00:02:40.987
A short practice routine.

00:02:41.787 --> 00:02:46.873
Ten to twenty minutes a day is enough to make steady progress. A simple plan:

00:02:47.673 --> 00:02:54.790
First: warm-up, 2 minutes: long, soft notes on B, A and G. Listen to your sound.

00:02:55.590 --> 00:03:01.211
Second: tonguing, 2 minutes: “du du du du” on different notes, at an even speed.

00:03:02.011 --> 00:03:07.364
Third: new note or difficult passage, 5 minutes: slowly, many times.

00:03:08.164 --> 00:03:13.864
Fourth: songs, 5 to 10 minutes: play the songs you know, and finish with your favourite.

00:03:14.664 --> 00:03:20.259
Stop before your lips or fingers get tired. Practising tired leads to bad habits.

00:03:21.759 --> 00:03:23.246
When something goes wrong.

00:03:24.046 --> 00:03:30.475
Squeaks: blowing too hard, or a hole not quite covered. Play softer and check your finger pads.

00:03:31.275 --> 00:03:36.556
The sound gets weak or bubbly: water has collected in the windway. See below.

00:03:37.356 --> 00:03:44.845
Low notes do not sound: a small leak somewhere. Check every hole, especially 6 and 7, and blow more gently.

00:03:45.645 --> 00:03:50.245
High notes do not sound: check the thumb pinch, and use slightly faster air.

00:03:51.745 --> 00:03:52.787
Condensation.

00:03:53.587 --> 00:04:12.472
Your warm breath turns into water inside the cold recorder. When the windway gets wet the sound becomes weak or bubbly. To reduce it, warm the head joint in your hands or a pocket before playing. If it happens while you play, cover the window with a finger and blow a sharp puff of air into the windway to clear it.

00:04:13.972 --> 00:04:15.337
Cleaning and storing.

00:04:16.137 --> 00:04:21.487
After playing, take the recorder apart and let the pieces dry in the air before putting them away.

00:04:22.287 --> 00:04:28.065
Wipe the inside with a cleaning rod and a soft cloth, gently, without touching the window edge.

00:04:28.865 --> 00:04:39.187
A plastic recorder can be washed in lukewarm water with mild soap, then rinsed and left to dry. Wooden recorders must not be washed: follow the maker’s instructions.

00:04:39.987 --> 00:04:45.318
Keep the recorder in its case, away from heat, direct sun and very cold places.

00:04:46.118 --> 00:04:50.331
Do not share recorders without washing them, and never chew on the mouthpiece.

00:04:51.831 --> 00:04:52.416
Tuning.

00:04:53.216 --> 00:05:06.333
If you play with others or with recordings, you may need to adjust your tuning. Pulling the head joint out a little makes the recorder sound slightly lower; pushing it in makes it higher. Use a tuner app on the note A to check.

00:05:07.133 --> 00:05:16.144
Tip: Be patient with yourself. Every recorder player has squeaked and struggled with the low C. A few minutes every day is the secret.

00:05:17.644 --> 00:05:18.376
Practice.

00:05:19.176 --> 00:05:23.098
First: follow the practice routine above every day for a week.

00:05:23.898 --> 00:05:28.479
Second: write down which songs you can play from start to finish without stopping.

00:05:29.279 --> 00:05:32.913
Third: clean your recorder after your last practice of the week.

00:05:34.713 --> 00:05:35.962
That's all for today.

00:05:36.762 --> 00:05:38.153
If you want to keep going:

00:05:38.953 --> 00:05:49.773
FlautaQuest is the app behind the ebook. It teaches the soprano recorder from zero, in short, step-by-step lessons, in the style of language-learning apps like Duolingo.

00:05:50.573 --> 00:05:53.479
Everything the ebook teaches is meant to go with it:

00:05:54.279 --> 00:05:56.514
holding the recorder and breathing well.

00:05:57.314 --> 00:05:59.204
the fingerings of each new note.

00:06:00.004 --> 00:06:01.593
reading simple music.

00:06:02.393 --> 00:06:05.340
learning your first songs, one step at a time.

00:06:06.140 --> 00:06:11.222
This ebook is free. Share it with anyone who wants to start playing the recorder.

00:06:12.022 --> 00:06:16.857
This episode of Play the Recorder from Zero was narrated with a synthetic voice.

